  "answer": "",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "By Definition 5.1, a³ ≡ a (mod 3) means 3 | (a³ − a).",
    "Use three case steps: a = 3k, a = 3k + 1, a = 3k + 2.",
    "It can help to factor first: a³ − a = (a − 1)a(a + 1). In each case one factor is a multiple of 3."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "We show $3 \\mid (a^3 - a)$. Note $a^3 - a = (a - 1)a(a + 1)$.",
    "Case $a = 3k$: $a^3 - a = (3k - 1)(3k)(3k + 1) = 3\\,k(3k - 1)(3k + 1)$.",
    "Case $a = 3k + 1$: $a^3 - a = (3k)(3k + 1)(3k + 2) = 3\\,k(3k + 1)(3k + 2)$.",
    "Case $a = 3k + 2$: $a^3 - a = (3k + 1)(3k + 2)(3k + 3) = 3(3k + 1)(3k + 2)(k + 1)$.",
    "In every case $3 \\mid (a^3 - a)$, so $a^3 \\equiv a \\pmod 3$. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "hammack-bop3",
    "section": "Chapter 7 exercises, #10 (p. 155)",
    "note": "This exact exercise, restated in ProofLab's words. Uses Definition 5.1 (congruence), Definition 4.4 (divides), and cases by remainder (the division algorithm)."
  },
